WEBVTT

1.1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.001
How would you sort a million names?

1.2
00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:05.204
Comparing everything with everything would take forever.

1.3
00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:09.264
There's a smarter way, and it starts with giving up on the big problem.

2.1
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:19.015
The first trick: split the list in half, then split each half again, and keep going until every piece holds just one number.

2.2
00:00:19.015 --> 00:00:21.313
A single number is already sorted.

3.1
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.616
The second trick is merging.

3.2
00:00:24.616 --> 00:00:33.788
If you have two lists that are already sorted, combining them into one sorted list is easy: you only ever compare the two front items.

4.1
00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:36.576
Watch the two pointers.

4.2
00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:41.721
Compare the front of each list, take the smaller one, and move that pointer forward.

4.3
00:00:41.721 --> 00:00:45.151
Repeat until everything has been copied into the result.

5.1
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:50.296
Six numbers, six steps, and the result is perfectly sorted.

5.2
00:00:50.296 --> 00:00:52.693
Each item is touched just once.

6.1
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:55.533
Now run it in reverse.

6.2
00:00:55.533 --> 00:01:04.459
Merge the single numbers into sorted pairs, merge pairs into sorted fours, and finally merge the two halves into the full sorted list.

7.1
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:07.175
Why is this fast?

7.2
00:01:07.175 --> 00:01:15.071
Halving a list can only happen about log n times, and each level of merging touches every item once.

7.3
00:01:15.071 --> 00:01:19.177
So the work grows like n log n, far below n squared.

8.1
00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:25.400
For a million items, that's about twenty million steps instead of a trillion.

8.2
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.911
The curves pull apart as the list grows.

9.1
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:34.000
So: split until trivial, then merge sorted pieces back together.

9.2
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.966
Hard problems get easy when you cut them small enough.

9.3
00:01:37.966 --> 00:01:41.564
That's how a million names get sorted in moments.
